    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d193">

                <pb facs="0297" n="289" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg289"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/14062</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Telegram Received by Mr. Lauchlin Currie, Administrative
                        Assistant to President Roosevelt </hi><anchor n="63" xml:id="d193-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-07-02">July 2, 1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dr. Lauchlin Currie</hi>: Generalissimo just
                    received following reliable information: Japan has already decided in near
                    future to abrogate Soviet–Japan Neutrality Pact. Afterwards she will declare war
                    on Russia. Japan now is only hoping that in this affair America will maintain
                    neutral attitude in which case she would take immediate action to attack Russia.
                    Please inform President.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Segac</hi>
                    </signed><anchor n="63a" xml:id="d193-f2"/>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d193-f1" n="63" type="source" xml:id="d193-f1-content">
                <p>Copy received in the Department probably on July 3.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d193-f2" n="63a" xml:id="d193-f2-content">
                <p>Probably the Naval Attaché for Air in China, Maj. James M. McHugh, U. S. M.
                    C.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
